1. INTRODUCTION
The Student Result Management System (SRMS) is a simple web-based application designed to manage students’ academic results efficiently. The system allows users to enter student details such as name, matriculation number, test score, and examination score. It then calculates the total score and assigns a grade automatically.
This project was developed using basic web technologies and follows the Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) methodology to ensure proper planning, design, implementation, testing, and maintenance.
2. SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT LIFE CYCLE (SDLC)
The Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) is a structured process used to design, develop, test, and maintain software systems. The SDLC phases applied in this project include:
Planning
Requirement Analysis
System Design
Implementation
Testing
Deployment
Maintenance
3. PLANNING PHASE
3.1 Project Objective
The main objective of the SRMS project is to:
Automate the process of calculating student results.
Reduce manual errors in result computation.
Provide a simple and user-friendly interface for result management.
3.2 Scope of the Project
The system will:
Accept student name and matric number.
Accept test and examination scores.
Calculate total score automatically.
Assign grades based on predefined rules.
Display results in a tabular format.
3.3 Tools and Technologies
HTML (Structure)
CSS (Styling)
JavaScript (Logic and functionality)
GitHub (Version control and hosting)
4. REQUIREMENT ANALYSIS
4.1 Functional Requirements
The system shall:
Allow input of student name.
Allow input of matriculation number.
Accept test and exam scores.
Calculate total score.
Assign grades automatically.
Display student results in a table.
4.2 Non-Functional Requirements
The system should be easy to use.
The interface should be simple and clear.
The system should respond quickly to user input.
The application should run on any modern web browser.
5. SYSTEM DESIGN
5.1 System Architecture
The SRMS uses a client-side architecture, where all operations are handled in the browser using JavaScript.
5.2 User Interface Design
The interface consists of:
A form for entering student data.
A button to submit results.
A table to display student name, matric number, total score, and grade.
5.3 Grade Computation Logic
Grades are assigned as follows:
70 and above → A
60 – 69 → B
50 – 59 → C
45 – 49 → D
Below 45 → F
6. IMPLEMENTATION
The system was implemented using:
HTML to structure the form and result table.
CSS to improve appearance and layout.
JavaScript to:
Capture form input
Calculate total score
Assign grades
Display results dynamically
The code was organized into files and uploaded to GitHub for version control and accessibility.
